    <GaugingStation location="Moyola New Bridge" name="Moyola" number="203020">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity-area station opened in January 1971.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Gauging section at the station always too deep for in-river gauging. Traditionally gauging carried out with cableway (now defunct). At high flows, river overspills minimally on left bank. Multi-arched bridge just downstream of station, between the piers, the inverts act as informal, horizontal controls. Rating derived from current meter gaugings. Simple extrapolation occurs above 2.5m. Three peak flow ratings across period of record.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity-area station located on right bank. Masonry hut housing stilling well with main instrument using pressure probe and backup using shaft encoder with float and weight. Channel width c.21m at station.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Daily flow data for the entire period of record was reprocessed in 2002. Flows from July 2002 are considered suspect due to construction work on the bridge downstream. The rating equation was adjusted in February 2008 because Roads Service filled the scoured invert with concrete. Water year 1976/1977 rejected due to missing data. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2021 (WINFAP Files v10) and in August 2025 (WINFAP Files v14).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Kilberry reservoir provides storage in catchment for public water supply, however its impact on the gauging station is minor.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Predominantly grassland and heath with limited afforestation. Urban areas at Magherafelt and Maghera but no major industry. Solid geology - north of the Omagh Thrust and west of the Tow Valley Fault, Dalradian rock types form a generally impermeable basement composed of metamorphosed, schistose sandstone, shale, limestone and basic igneous intrusive and extrusive lithologies. Moinian and Ordovician igneous complexes form generally impermeable basement to the south and east. Much of south is composed of Carboniferous conglomerate, sandstone, mudstone and limestone that are generally indurated. Drift geology - extensive glacial till and sand and gravel. Rock near surface and post-glacial peat cover on high ground with variable development of river alluvium in valleys.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">SPGI</Comment>
        </Comments>
